WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:09.860
PAUL PETERSON: I'm pleased to have with me, Jeb

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.870
Bush, the former governor of Florida.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:16.010
And the person who saw tremendous gains in student achievement

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.731
while he was governor of that state.

00:00:18.731 --> 00:00:20.480
And one of the things he did in that state

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.400
was introduce a lot of school choice.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.810
Whether it was school vouchers, or digital learning opportunities,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:33.620
or income tax credits that gave scholarships to low income students,

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.300
or charter schools.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.920
And of all the innovations that have occurred,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.020
the one that is really spreading most widely in the United States today

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:42.930
are charter schools.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.630
So I thought I would ask the governor a few questions.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.790
What do you think of charter schools, governor?

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.260
JEB BUSH: Well, I think they're--

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.350
charter schools, for those taking this course,

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.480
are, simply, independent public schools.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.510
They have to take all comers, but they're public schools.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.200
They just have a governance structure that is school based.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.620
In fact, in the day, not that long ago, people always

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:11.060
talked about neighborhood schools and local schools

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.480
being the driver of a successful education system.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.550
Somehow we've lost touch with that.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.050
But that's what charter schools are.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.100
They're run by, typically, passionate dedicated leaders.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.710
And if they don't work, there should be some, and typically, in most states,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.520
there's a process to close them down.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.640
Now my personal experience about charter schools didn't start as governor.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.900
I set up the first charter school with T Willard Fair, who's

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.710
the longest serving director of the Urban League Chapter,

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.110
of any Urban League Chapter.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.085
And we built the Literacy Charter School in 1997.

00:01:45.085 --> 00:01:46.460
PAUL PETERSON: Oh, where is that?

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:47.450
JEB BUSH: In Miami.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.450
PAUL PETERSON: In Miami.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.450
JEB BUSH: And it was the first school.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.710
There were four schools that were created.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.231
We had 90 kids.

00:01:55.231 --> 00:01:57.230
It was one of the hardest things I've ever done.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.880
There was real resistance by the school board.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.900
We were forced to accept our contract in effect.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.020
And you would've thought we were the greatest threat to the fourth largest

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.311
school district in the country.

00:02:07.311 --> 00:02:09.710
These 90 children being taught, in uniform,

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:12.350
where parents signed a contract that said that they

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:13.880
would volunteer in the school.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.310
PAUL PETERSON: But you were a cloud on a distant sky.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.920
The sky was blue, but there was that little cloud out there.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.690
You never knew how big it could grow.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.398
JEB BUSH: So over time we changed the law

00:02:22.398 --> 00:02:24.980
to make it easier for charter schools to be set up.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.010
Many states, affective states, that have this

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.800
have a statewide authorization of charter schools.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.330
We don't.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.070
That was ruled unconstitutional in our state.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.240
We tried that.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:41.720
But now, fast forward from 1997, we now have 260,000 charter school students.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:45.380
Many high performing schools, some that aren't as high performing.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.260
And the beauty of charter schools is, if it doesn't work,

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.710
there is an obligation, I think, a duty to close them down.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:55.370
They do this against all odds because they, typically, don't

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.010
get the same capital requirements.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:03.030
And many times you can't share the space with the traditional public schools.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.370
But I think it's a good thing because in a perfect system, the innovation that

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:11.540
takes place in any school ought to be applied in other schools.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.420
The beauty of charter schools is they can innovate and try different things.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.620
Unfortunately, the traditional schools don't embrace those changes the way

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.920
you would expect.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.470
And they feel threatened by high performing charter schools.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.890
PAUL PETERSON: There are, around the country,

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.480
some really high performing charter schools.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.810
Whether it's in New York, or Boston, Washington DC, New Orleans, Los

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.510
Angeles.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.530
So why is it that there's so much opposition to these high

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.220
performing charter schools?

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.650
JEB BUSH: Well, I don't think this is a conjecture on my part.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.490
Monopolies are different than open systems.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.550
Monopolies protect the status quo.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.060
And government run monopolies protect it even more because they have more power.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.780
And politicized, unionized, government run monopolies, there

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.470
are 13,000 of them in the country, resist change.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.980
PAUL PETERSON: Why doesn't the public vote in a school

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.050
board that embraces the change?

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.410
JEB BUSH: Some places, they do.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:15.050
In some places you have very progressive superintendents.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:22.079
But that is not the norm because the system itself resists change.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.690
And that's why charters, they flourish.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.500
But it requires political leadership, and vigilance, to make sure

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.540
that they're allowed to participate and grow.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:32.370
Ultimately, scale matters.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:37.460
So if you have success academies, and in other charter management organizations

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.360
in New York State and City, for example, that have lights out test results,

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.830
irrefutable gains amongst low income kids,

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.680
taking kids that are lagging way behind and bringing them

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:53.000
up to averages that are as good as any school in the entire state.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.980
That's what sustains these schools.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.020
When you create a scale of parents that demand the quality school,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.480
and you create a constituency around it.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.510
But, in politics, a lot of times the adults

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:09.930
are the ones that the special interests care about.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.350
And the adults in the system are the largest employers

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.360
in almost every county in the United States.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.950
And so they wield big power.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.050
But if you can get a constituency of moms and dads

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.050
that believe in these schools, and they do,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.487
then they can be the counterweight to union leadership

00:05:28.487 --> 00:05:30.320
that has disproportionate power politically.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.278
PAUL PETERSON: Some of the leading civil rights

00:05:32.278 --> 00:05:34.770
organizations have expressed opposition to charter schools,

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.534
saying they should not be expanded.

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:37.950
JEB BUSH: The NAACP has done that.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.270
PAUL PETERSON: And yet, charter schools are serving the black community more

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.810
than any other communities.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:48.910
So, I'm always curious about why that might be the case.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.700
JEB BUSH: Well, it shouldn't be the case.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.680
Rising student achievement is the civil rights issue of our time.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.160
And you would think a venerable organization, like the NAACP,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.670
would be on the side of children, and the families that want to get out

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.665
of failing schools and win the lottery.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.170
Charter schools don't pick who they wanted.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:11.800
They're not creaming off the top.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.090
They're providing an alternative that parents desperately want.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:20.850
And you would think an organization with a incredible history, like the NAACP,

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:21.990
would be on their side.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.340
But they're not.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:27.830
And they're not for reasons I don't quite understand.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:33.630
PAUL PETERSON: So despite opposition, especially from the teacher union,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.500
it is the case that 6% of our students are attending

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.490
charter schools around the country.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.635
And it continues to grow every year.

00:06:41.635 --> 00:06:48.300
Do you see a day when we're going to have a charter school

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.640
educational system?

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.020
JEB BUSH: I hope so.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.385
I think there will be a point in time, and in some places like New Orleans,

00:06:56.385 --> 00:06:57.510
we've already reached this.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.910
Washington DC, it's been reached.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:04.890
Where the scale, basically, means that you're not turning back.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.370
That there'll be a melding of interests.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:13.710
That public education will be defined by educating students.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:18.300
And there will be choices for parents both public, and private, and hybrids,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.680
both using online learning, and competency based learning,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.110
and course choice.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.510
They're going to basically get a menu of services,

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:31.020
and they can choose from it to meet the best the means

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.900
by which their child can learn.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:35.760
That's where we're moving.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.620
There's deep resistance to it.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:43.830
But we would get far faster further if we moved to that basis.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:49.255
Every successful enterprise empowers people to make choices.

00:07:49.255 --> 00:07:51.060
That's called progress.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:55.130
And when there's a tension, and when there's a choice, there's competition,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.995
there's innovation, there's continuous improvement.

00:08:01.027 --> 00:08:01.860
I mean, it's a fact.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.300
It's not conjecture, on my part, in that particular case.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.052
And education would benefit from this.

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:08.010
PAUL PETERSON: So I know you have a foundation,

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.740
The Foundation For Educational Excellence.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.577
Is your foundation supporting the expansion of charter schools?

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:15.660
Is that one of your goals?

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.600
JEB BUSH: Sure, we support the whole array of school choice,

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.570
including charter schools, which is the largest option.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.420
And we support accountability as well.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:29.520
I think the combination of higher expectations, rewarding progress,

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.080
and rewarding excellence directly to the school combined with empowering parents

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.750
to make decisions, with quality teachers at the lead,

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.904
will yield a better result. Florida has done this.

00:08:41.904 --> 00:08:44.820
And we've gone from the bottom to having some of the greatest learning

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:46.000
gains of any state.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.230
PAUL PETERSON: So you would have charter schools,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.730
but you'd hold them accountable?

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:51.630
JEB BUSH: Yeah.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.220
PAUL PETERSON: Students have to take these tests.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.595
They have to show that they're doing well on these tests.

00:08:55.595 --> 00:08:58.380
If they don't do well, then you close them?

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.100
JEB BUSH: Yeah, look.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.780
The idea, this is not to hurt traditional public schools.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:10.140
This is to give parents choices so that quality improves across the board.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:13.530
And if a traditional public school's not working that should be closed down,

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.100
if it's a chronic failure, and if a charter school miss appropriates money,

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:23.130
or has lousy outcomes for students, why would we want to keep it open?

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.870
Of course not.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:28.290
Everything needs to be focused on one basic point, which

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:32.070
is a child is deserving of a year's worth of knowledge in a year's time.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.857
And it should happen for 12 years.

00:09:34.857 --> 00:09:36.690
And a child should graduate from high school

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.020
with a piece of paper that actually has value, which means that they are

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.940
college ready and, or, career ready.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:46.290
In all honesty, Paul, based on your research and other people's research,

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:50.130
that I admire and respect, effectively, a third of our kids are college and,

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.570
or, career ready.

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:55.380
We better start training a lot of things to be able to improve them.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:59.190
PAUL PETERSON: Well that is an disconcerting fact you just reported,

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:03.240
but nonetheless you've given us hope that the charter school

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.860
innovation will help turn that around.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.270
Thank you, very much.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:12.060
I've been speaking with Governor Jeb Bush, Governor of Florida.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.720
In the period when the state of Florida was making the fastest

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.950
progress in American education.

